    reset : BOOL;
END_VAR

VAR_OUTPUT
    average : LREAL;
    windowSizeReached : BOOL;
    error : BOOL;
    status : WORD;
END_VAR

VAR
    dataBuffer : ARRAY[1..100] OF LREAL;
    sum : LREAL;
    count : INT;
    index : INT;
    lastTrigger : BOOL;
    lastReset : BOOL;
    isInitialized : BOOL := FALSE;
END_VAR

// Main logic
// Reset handling
IF reset THEN
    // Reset all variables
    sum := 0.0;
    count := 0;
    index := 0;
    lastTrigger := FALSE;
    average := 0.0;
    windowSizeReached := FALSE;
    error := FALSE;
    status := 16#0000;
    isInitialized := FALSE;
END_IF
```

The reset logic clears all state variables and sets the function block back to its initial condition. When cyclicExecution is enabled, I validate the windowSize parameter—if it's outside the valid range of 1 to 100, I set an error flag and return the appropriate status code.

Once initialized, I add the new value to the buffer at the current index, update the running sum, and increment the count. When the buffer reaches its windowSize limit, I stop adding new values and maintain the full window instead.

For the trigger-based approach, I check for a rising edge by comparing the current trigger state to the previous one. If detected and initialization has already occurred, I process the new value the same way—storing it, updating the sum, and advancing the index.

When the count reaches the window size, I shift the oldest value out by subtracting it from the sum and set windowSizeReached to TRUE to indicate the moving average window is complete. The average itself is calculated by dividing the accumulated sum by the current count, with error handling for division by zero.

The function maintains state across executions by storing previous trigger values, an array of data points up to the window size, and variables tracking the running sum and current index position. This allows the moving average calculation to work continuously as new values arrive through either cyclic execution or trigger-based inputs.

